# i18n Vue Rule

## Description
This rule ensures that all text content in Vue files is properly internationalized using i18n translations.

## Rule Details
- All text content in Vue templates must use the `$t()` function
- All text content in script sections must use the `t()` function from `useI18n()`
- All translations must be defined in both `en-US.ts` and `fr-FR.ts` locale files
- No hardcoded strings should be present in Vue files

## Examples
❌ Incorrect:
```vue
<template>
  <h1>Welcome to our app</h1>
  <p>This is a description</p>
</template>
```

✅ Correct:
```vue
<template>
  <h1>{{ $t('welcome.title') }}</h1>
  <p>{{ $t('welcome.description') }}</p>
</template>

<script setup>
import { useI18n } from 'vue-i18n'
const { t } = useI18n()
</script>
```

## Translation Files
All translations must be added to both locale files:

```typescript
// en-US.ts
export default {
  welcome: {
    title: 'Welcome to our app',
    description: 'This is a description'
  }
}

// fr-FR.ts
export default {
  welcome: {
    title: 'Bienvenue sur notre application',
    description: 'Ceci est une description'
  }
}
```

## Exceptions
- Component names
- Technical identifiers
- CSS class names
- HTML attributes that don't need translation (e.g., `data-testid`)
- URLs and paths
- Numbers and dates (unless they need specific formatting)

## Implementation Steps
1. Identify all text content in Vue files
2. Create translation keys in both locale files
3. Replace hardcoded text with `$t()` or `t()` function calls
4. Ensure all translations are properly defined in both languages
5. Test the application in both languages to verify translations
